
Source File (CSV/Excel) → Python Conversion → Parquet + TOC File → CDM Quarantine Bucket (GCS) → BigQuery S_ Table
CDM Next is a pre-installed framework on the server; the developer's job is to prepare the right input files and trigger the process correctly �.
Step-by-Step Developer Workflow
Step 1: Understand the Source
Identify your SOR (System of Record) — e.g., NEW, CMOD, HCLT �.
Know the source file format (CSV or Excel), field names, and delimiter (default is comma; Excel needs no delimiter — Pandas reads it natively) �.
Step 2: Raise a DRT ID
Go to the DRT (Data Registration Tool) portal and raise a request for your data source �.
The request goes for approval (to Kathy or the designated approver).
Once approved, you receive a DRT ID — this is mandatory before development begins �.
This ID is cross-validated by CDM Next against the Data Plex at runtime; mismatches cause load failures �.
Step 3: Create the Three Configuration JSON Files
These are the core inputs to the Python conversion code �:
Step 4: Run the Python Conversion Code
The Python code reads the source file using Pandas, applies the source-to-target field name mapping, converts fields to BigQuery-compatible data types using the schema JSON, and generates two output files �:
Parquet file — the data payload (with schema attached matching BQ target)
TOC (Table of Contents) file — metadata file containing DRT ID, pipeline type (incremental), object name, object format (Parquet), CDM landing type (GCS), target type (BQ), target path, write disposition (right_append or right_truncate), record count, column count, business date �
The Parquet and TOC files must share the same file name, differing only in extension �.
Step 5: Run the CDM Driver Shell Script
A shell script wrapper calls the pre-installed CDM Next Python script �.
The script scans the base output folder, detects paired Parquet + TOC files, and pushes them to the GCS quarantine bucket �.
CDM Next then validates the DRT ID against Data Plex, checks record counts, checks schema compatibility, and loads data into the S_ (sanitized) table in BigQuery within 5–10 minutes �.
Step 6: Identify the Correct Target Dataset
Confirm with the data modeler and product owner which dataset the data belongs to �:
Servicing data → servicing_non_SAR_sanitized
Organization data → org_non_SAR_sanitized
Capital Markets → capital_markets_non_SAR_sanitized
SAR-sanitized datasets exist but are only used for restricted data (HCL data does not qualify) �.
Step 7: Create the BigQuery DDL and Deploy via Harness
Write a standard DDL (no partition columns — CDM Next does not support loading into partitioned tables) �.
Deploy the DDL via Harness (team members familiar with Harness can assist) �.
To query today's data post-load, filter using HDS_LOAD_TIMESTAMP or HDS_BUSINESS_DATE since no partitions are available �.
Step 8: Set Up Data Plex (Aspect Binding + PII Tagging)
Two separate JSON files must be created for Data Plex setup �:
Aspect Binding JSON — registers the DRT ID against the target table in Data Plex (enables CDM cross-validation)
Policy Tag Taxonomy JSON — defines PII columns (e.g., borrower_SSN, co_borrower_SSN) with confidentiality type (con_PII or con_PII_date)
⚠️ Important distinction: Tagging in Data Plex only marks a field as PII visually in the schema. Actual data encryption/masking only happens when you apply the Policy Tag Taxonomy at the BigQuery table level �. Both files are needed for full PII compliance.
Step 9: Repository Split — What Goes Where
Key Validations CDM Next Performs Automatically
DRT ID in TOC file must match Data Plex registration �
Parquet schema must match the target BQ table schema (data types + nullable flags) �
Record count in TOC file is reconciled against rows loaded into BQ �
Control file (for ABs / data governance): expects aggregate controls like record count and unpaid balance amount — plan for this in scope or Phase 2 �
